# english-everyday-conversations.kb - English / Everyday conversations
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 6 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.

small talk | definition | Small talk is conversation with no real content, and its purpose is to keep the contact going rather than to exchange information. That is not a criticism, because it does the work of establishing a relationship before anything harder can be attempted. The subjects that work are weather, traffic, and anything happening in the room, and the subjects that fail are politics, money and other people. The rules are unwritten and learnable only by watching what people steer back to. | small talk, weather, polite, contact, relationship, steer | what is small talk, how to make small talk, small talk topics to avoid | small talk | small talk
catch up | definition | To catch up means to bring yourself current after a gap, and the phrase covers news, on people, and on lost time, all with the same sense of a debt being cleared. Asking to catch up is a normal way to reopen a conversation with someone you have not seen, and it is more specific than how are you because it asks about the gap. Catching up with work means the same thing in miniature, and people who never do it accumulate a backlog rather than a single debt. | catch up, current, gap, news, reopen, backlog | what does catch up mean, how to catch up with someone, catch up with work | catch up | catch up
weekend | definition | The weekend is the part of the week from roughly Friday evening to Sunday evening, and the exact boundary varies by employer and by country. Its word is plural, which reflects that it covers two days rather than one period, and that is a small clue about what people mean by it. The weekend also carries a strong cultural weight, since it is the main period in which people see family and friends, and everything from opening hours to transport is arranged around it. | weekend, friday, sunday, period, hours, arrange | when does the weekend start, is weekend plural, what happens on a weekend | weekend | weekend
weather chat | definition | Talking about the weather is the most universally safe opening in a conversation, because everybody has an opinion and nobody can be wrong. It is also the fallback topic people retreat to when they want to avoid a question, and a host who steers every talk to the forecast is usually doing that on purpose. The linguistic interest is that English treats weather as uncountable, where a language like Tagalog or Bisaya counts rain and wind as separate words, which changes what each one can be used to describe. | weather chat, forecast, safe topic, conversation, uncountable, opinion | how to talk about the weather, is weather small talk, why is weather safe to discuss | weather chat | weather talk
opinion | definition | An opinion is a belief held without the evidence that would make it a fact, and the word is neutral about whether that is good or bad. English marks the difference sharply, being fact, opinion, belief and rumour, where many languages use one word for all of them. This is why the phrase in my opinion exists, because it marks the claim as the speaker's own and not the listener's obligation. Being wrong about an opinion is not an error the way being wrong about a fact is. | opinion, belief, fact, evidence, rumour, claim | what is an opinion, difference between fact and opinion, is in my opinion a claim | opinion | opinion
rumour | definition | A rumour is a piece of information passed on without being checked, and the word carries the doubt openly, so a rumour is a claim whose source the speaker is not vouching for. English is unusually honest here, offering gossip, hearsay and rumour with slightly different shades, and none of them claims to be verified. What makes the word necessary is that unchecked information travels fast, and marking it keeps people from passing it on as fact. | rumour, unchecked, gossip, hearsay, verified, travel | what is a rumour, difference between rumour and gossip, is hearsay the same as rumour | rumour | tsismis
